# STAR EC2 box — Caddyfile (STARProject gate only).
#
# The EC2 box runs the central `auth` login service (the cookie minter) and now
# STARProject. This Caddy sits in front of STARProject and applies the same
# forward_auth gate the apps machine uses, calling the local auth's /verify so a
# signed-in .starberkeley.org cookie is required. auth.starberkeley.org itself
# stays tunnel-direct to auth:5000 (not fronted here) — gating it would deadlock.
#
# TLS: Cloudflare terminates it at the edge and cloudflared speaks plain HTTP to
# Caddy, so set SCHEME=http (Caddy binds :80, issues no certificates). The tunnel
# routes project.starberkeley.org → http://caddy:80.

# Gate a site on a valid session. Import first in each protected site block.
(protected) {
	# Strip any X-Auth-* the *client* sent before the app can see it. forward_auth
	# overwrites these from the /verify response on the happy path, so this is
	# belt-and-braces -- but on the apps machine X-Auth-Email now decides which
	# user's designs you may edit, so a client must never get to propose one.
	# Kept identical here so the two Caddyfiles do not drift.
	#
	# route {} is required, not cosmetic: the Caddyfile adapter otherwise re-sorts
	# by built-in directive order, placing request_header AFTER forward_auth — which
	# would delete the X-Auth-* headers forward_auth just set, handing the app a
	# request with no identity. Wrapping keeps the strip ahead of the gate.
	route {
		request_header -X-Auth-Email
		request_header -X-Auth-User

		forward_auth {$AUTH_UPSTREAM:auth:5000} {
			uri /verify
			# Identity for the upstream app, taken from the /verify response.
			copy_headers X-Auth-Email X-Auth-User
			# Strip hop-by-hop upgrade headers from the /verify subrequest only (the
			# auth service 400s on them); the real WebSocket upgrade still proxies
			# through untouched.
			header_up -Connection
			header_up -Upgrade
		}
	}
}

# ── STARProject ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Single container: Next.js serves both its pages and /api routes, so one
# reverse_proxy (no /api split). Postgres is the starproject-db service.
#
# /api/mcp is the MCP endpoint for agents. They have no browser session, so it
# skips the cookie gate and the app authenticates it itself with a per-user
# bearer token (starproject/src/app/api/mcp/route.ts). Strip X-Auth-* there so a
# token is the only identity that path can carry. Same shape as analytics
# /api/ingest below: one route {} so the order written is the order run, and
# deploy/caddy/tests/check_gate_order.py lists /api/mcp as public by design.
{$SCHEME:http}://project.{$BASE_DOMAIN:starberkeley.org} {
	route {
		handle /api/mcp* {
			request_header -X-Auth-Email
			request_header -X-Auth-User
			reverse_proxy starproject:3000
		}
		import protected
		reverse_proxy starproject:3000
	}
}

# ── Server analytics ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The hub serves the panel and its API on one port. Everything is cookie-gated
# except /api/ingest: the agents push there and have no browser session, so the
# hub authenticates that path itself with a per-host bearer token. The panel
# then admits only STARProject admins (server-analytics/internal/hub/admins.go).
#
# Same shape as the apps machine's parts.* site: the whole body sits in one
# route {} so the order written is the order run. Without it the adapter sorts
# handle ahead of the `import protected` route and nothing is gated.
# deploy/caddy/tests/check_gate_order.py checks this file too.
{$SCHEME:http}://analytics.{$BASE_DOMAIN:starberkeley.org} {
	route {
		handle /api/ingest {
			request_header -X-Auth-Email
			request_header -X-Auth-User
			reverse_proxy analytics:8080
		}
		import protected
		reverse_proxy analytics:8080
	}
}
